#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110346 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110612 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "637d4fe0-abb6-5050-a541-f132e78f9b8a" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When the Perfect Square is Isolated"}] :question/id #uuid "34bac64f-5afc-5c30-bb87-15b3df40f8a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-14817" :question/problem "Solve $-2(3k-12)^2 = 0.$" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(3k-12)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (3k-12)^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{(3k-12)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{0} \\\\ |3k-12| & =0 \\\\ 3k-12 & =0 \\\\ 3k & =12 \\\\ k & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110348 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110613 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2aca8019-3239-539c-b7a9-e1a9ca461a61" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Parameter"}] :question/id #uuid "b9db498b-f1e3-5d52-b23d-106895586815" :question/math-academy-id "e-14820" :question/problem "Which value of $k$ gives precisely one solution to the equation $(x+3)^2+10=k?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+10 & =k \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =k-10 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x+3)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{k-10} \\\\ |x+3| & =\\sqrt{k-10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation has only one solution if the right-hand side is equal to zero. So, we solve for the value of $k$ that makes the right-hand side equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{k-10} & =0 \\\\ k-10 & =0 \\\\ k & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=10$ will give only one solution to the equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110349 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110614 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f637b472-4c1a-511b-9820-3e07f659eb25" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Perfect Square"}] :question/id #uuid "281c39c4-7b52-51aa-aad8-dbd40223b796" :question/math-academy-id "e-14822" :question/problem "Given that $g^2 -18g + 81 = 0$, what are the values of $g?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the left-hand side is a perfect square. It just needs to be factored:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{2}-18g+81 & =0 \\\\ {g}^{2}-2\\cdot 9\\cdot g+{9}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (g-9)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(g-9)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{0} \\\\ |g-9| & =0 \\\\ g-9 & =0 \\\\ g & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110350 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110615 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02785337-efe0-54cb-9433-46ff4a9c6302" :knowledge-point/title "Equations With No Real Solutions"}] :question/id #uuid "1e0655ad-122c-5d94-8f8b-9e476eaf4d78" :question/math-academy-id "e-14824" :question/problem "Consider the equation $(4x+1)^2 +2=3k.$ For which values of $k$ does this equation have no real solutions?" :question/worked-solution "Let's try and solve using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4x+1)^{2}+2 & =3k \\\\ (4x+1)^{2} & =3k-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{(4x+1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{3k-2} \\\\ |4x+1| & =\\sqrt{3k-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation will have no real solutions if the expression under the square root is negative, which gives the following condition:\n\n$$\n3k-2 < 0\n$$\n\nWe solve this inequality for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3k-2 & <0 \\\\ 3k & <2 \\\\ k & <\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, any value of $k$ that is less than $\\dfrac 2 3$ will give an equation with no real solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110613 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2aca8019-3239-539c-b7a9-e1a9ca461a61" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Parameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "041e724a-4ddb-5e58-90c2-60a8c0f44fee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533142} {:answer/id #uuid "1c93cda4-0807-53ea-a5d6-c1559bb51bc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533209} {:answer/id #uuid "9647c513-d055-5724-a06b-77925df0a593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533503} {:answer/id #uuid "9e5201a0-d283-524b-8d15-3fcaaaa19246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533523} {:answer/id #uuid "b3edbdbd-ec90-5bcd-a793-beaa48e8be0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e5201a0-d283-524b-8d15-3fcaaaa19246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533523} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5c722ad-44b6-5d52-bb78-d885d9d06d7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "306bf2f9-520c-5fa5-bb34-4cf2b1fae3a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-111795" :question/problem "Which value of $q$ gives precisely one solution to the equation ${x}^{2}+q+3=7?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+q+3 & =7 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =7-q-3 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4-q \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{4-q} \\\\ |x| & =\\sqrt{4-q}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation has only one solution if the right-hand side is equal to zero, and the right-hand side is zero only if the expression underneath the radical is zero. So, we solve for the value of $q$ that makes the expression under the radical equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-q & =0 \\\\ q & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q=4$ will give only one solution to the equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110613 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2aca8019-3239-539c-b7a9-e1a9ca461a61" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Parameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "782f1c18-62da-597f-ac6a-ecc309346025" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533423} {:answer/id #uuid "aeaba178-c4f9-5f80-886d-7bfe5c72e6eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533559} {:answer/id #uuid "b16c83d5-f9f0-5284-959b-e164947c839e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533568} {:answer/id #uuid "c05a90b1-3851-524e-b017-09d23e1cde34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533603} {:answer/id #uuid "fb3d8c4a-7d57-5058-9ead-bd693452cc53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c05a90b1-3851-524e-b017-09d23e1cde34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533603} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ff7defe-5d2a-5d0e-a412-4995a08ebb39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c1d7b383-1862-5cf5-aff4-6a1ed42adc9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-111797" :question/problem "Which value of $k$ gives precisely one solution to the equation ${t}^{2}+k-8=-2?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we solve for $t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+k-8 & =-2 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =-2-k+8 \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{6-k} \\\\ |t| & =\\sqrt{6-k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation has only one solution if the right-hand side is equal to zero, and the right-hand side is zero only if the expression underneath the radical is zero. So, we solve for the value of $k$ that makes the expression under the radical equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-k & =0 \\\\ k & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=6$ will give only one solution to the equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110613 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2aca8019-3239-539c-b7a9-e1a9ca461a61" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Parameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "259f42a6-bc99-586c-a899-1aec20f8acd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533234} {:answer/id #uuid "7fe9f2f0-3ee7-5eed-9878-14df14b06ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533442} {:answer/id #uuid "903ed28d-ca35-56ad-8a32-039d529d866c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533487} {:answer/id #uuid "d014b300-40ba-556a-8389-c84731456411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533647} {:answer/id #uuid "ea04fdc0-7467-527f-9fab-5c03cef45eeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fe9f2f0-3ee7-5eed-9878-14df14b06ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533442} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a6abe80-3c2d-572d-80a8-2e56881206aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9dc4d198-f79f-5ff0-bdf7-8f507199b081" :question/math-academy-id "q-111798" :question/problem "Which value of $k$ gives precisely one solution to the equation  ${x}^{2}+8=k?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+8 & =k \\\\ {x}^{2} & =k-8 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{k-8} \\\\ |x| & =\\sqrt{k-8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation has only one solution if the right-hand side is equal to zero, and the right-hand side is zero only if the expression underneath the radical is zero. So, we solve for the value of $k$ that makes the expression under the radical equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k-8 & =0 \\\\ k & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=8$ will give only one solution to the equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534085 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110614 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f637b472-4c1a-511b-9820-3e07f659eb25" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Perfect Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49353a8c-8802-52d1-9ed3-d0af365872e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533310} {:answer/id #uuid "4fab41a6-0bdb-57b1-8a90-0088e0974be6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533329} {:answer/id #uuid "c666e615-c02f-56aa-b316-8da522b6eea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533616} {:answer/id #uuid "eff16410-8ee2-5b35-9cd1-86c63ed51d55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533728} {:answer/id #uuid "f0ce4430-b6d5-5611-8ce7-55aa1ae50344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533732}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49353a8c-8802-52d1-9ed3-d0af365872e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533310} :answer-field/id #uuid "71992ead-ebe0-5657-9851-46873c0fbeed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eec9dcf0-bc54-564e-8800-178ded8aabf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-111841" :question/problem "Given that $9{t}^{2}+30t+25=0,$ what are the values of $t?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the left-hand side is a perfect square. It just needs to be factored:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{t}^{2}+30t+25 & =0 \\\\ (3t)^{2}+2\\cdot 3t\\cdot 5+{5}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (3t+5)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(3t+5)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{0} \\\\ |3t+5| & =0 \\\\ 3t+5 & =0 \\\\ 3t & =-5 \\\\ t & =-\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110614 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f637b472-4c1a-511b-9820-3e07f659eb25" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Perfect Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31591f02-2246-5a37-997d-d58956049390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533259} {:answer/id #uuid "5d34b7a2-4375-5f19-a991-91ab0913d1e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533351} {:answer/id #uuid "7fe183aa-8a1f-5e7